          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "The risk check rated your description **routine**, meaning it did not match the emergency red-flag table, but that is not a clearance. The reference on coronary artery disease describes this exact pattern, pressure or heaviness brought on by exertion and relieved by rest, as classic stable angina. It is a reason to see a clinician soon, not to wait for the next routine visit. Only a clinician can tell whether it is angina.\n\n**Call emergency services now** if any of these happen, because the red-flag guidance treats them as a possible heart attack until proven otherwise:\n- Chest pressure, tightness, squeezing or heaviness that lasts more than a few minutes, or that goes away and comes back.\n- Discomfort spreading to the arms, shoulders, neck, jaw, back or upper abdomen.\n- Chest discomfort with breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea, vomiting, light-headedness, or a sense of impending doom.\n- Chest discomfort at rest, or brought on by much less effort than before.\n\nThe general reference on coronary artery disease also warns that waiting to see whether it settles is a common and costly mistake. If you have diabetes, known heart disease, or several heart risk factors, the red-flag guidance says any chest discomfort should be checked promptly.\n\n**After a diagnosis.** The secondary-prevention guidance treats prevention as a package whose benefit comes from using the elements together. It lists long-term antiplatelet treatment for most people, with duration weighed against bleeding risk; high-intensity statin therapy for established coronary disease; blood pressure and cholesterol management; smoking cessation, which it calls the largest single modifiable factor; cardiac rehabilitation, a supervised exercise and education program; yearly influenza vaccination; and a review at least once a year, or after symptom changes. The general reference adds that symptom-control medicines, and stenting or bypass surgery where symptoms persist, are part of the wider picture, and that a procedure relieves symptoms but does not replace the medicines underneath. The coronary reference did not retrieve this long-term plan, so ask your clinician what applies to you.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** The sources show disagreement on numbers, not on the overall approach. For cholesterol, US guidance describes statin intensity and percentage reduction from baseline, while European guidance sets explicit LDL goals, sometimes with a second medicine, so \"at goal\" means different things under each. For blood pressure, the US diagnostic threshold is 130/80 mm Hg, while European guidance uses 140/90 mm Hg and treats 130–139 / 80–89 as high-normal.\n\n**Limits.** The retrieved passages name no specific medicines or doses, and I have not given any. They do not grade the strength of evidence for each recommendation. The only strength claim I can make is that cardiac rehabilitation is described as strong relative to how rarely it is used. For grades, the original guidelines would need checking.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
